A||N||7||Eriamjh||Control Arms: Replace, rebush, reball?||||1||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||11-18-2001||09:08 AM||||According to the Hanes Fiero manual, the front upper control arms in an '84-'87 must be replaced when the bushings are toast.  This obviously cannot be true since they sell polyuerothane bushing for the Fieros at the FieroStore.com.  <P>Has anyone installed these bushings?  <P>In the factory manual, the lower ball joint is welded to the front lower control arm and the whole assembly must be replaced when the ball joint dies.  I see they sell lower ball joints for the '87s.  Has anyone had trouble getting these parts replaced?<P>My '87 with 155K miles need extensive bushing and ball joint work.  It's cheaper to rebush and replace the ball joints than buy all new arms all around (the front).  Has anyone done this?  Am I getting myself into a lot of trouble here?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||11-18-2001||09:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eriamjh:<BR><B>According to the Hanes Fiero manual, the front upper control arms in an '84-'87 must be replaced when the bushings are toast.  This obviously cannot be true since they sell polyuerothane bushing for the Fieros at the FieroStore.com.  <P>Has anyone installed these bushings?  <P>In the factory manual, the lower ball joint is welded to the front lower control arm and the whole assembly must be replaced when the ball joint dies.  I see they sell lower ball joints for the '87s.  Has anyone had trouble getting these parts replaced?<P>My '87 with 155K miles need extensive bushing and ball joint work.  It's cheaper to rebush and replace the ball joints than buy all new arms all around (the front).  Has anyone done this?  Am I getting myself into a lot of trouble here?<P>Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have poly bushings installed. They work very well. Handling and turning feel more positive. You do need to grease them well when installing otherwise they squeek.<P>To replace the front lower ball joints you need to use a small grinder. Grind off the 2 weld points and press the old ball joint out. Then press in the new one.<P>I did my suspension 2 years ago. I replaced bushings, ball joints, tie rod ends, new lowering springs, shocks, and struts. I did all the work. It's not bad to do but it is time consuming.<P><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||11-18-2001||11:32 AM||||To put in polly upper control arm bushings I feel is easyer than putting in stock style rubbers, cause the polly uses the stock outer sleve, so you don't have to try and get it out of the arm.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||11-18-2001||05:18 PM||||Wow,never looked into the poly for the front,but if I'd known that the outer shell from the old part could be left there,I definately would've just done that.Those things are a pain to install;took mine to a shop that had an experienced press operator,and they grumbled,charged me $60.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||11-18-2001||05:54 PM||||Bushings are easy to replace.  I heated mine up with a torch to make the glue release, then pushed them out with a big screwdriver.  Clean the housings well with a wire brush and grease them with the supplied grease.  I finally put grease fittings in mine, and it works fairly well.  Rides a bit better now.  Doesn't squeak as bad in the cold weather, either.<P>My lower balljoints (86 GT) weren't welded.  Still a PITA to press them out, though.<P>Get the Moog Problem Solver upper balljoints.  You won't be sorry you did.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||11-18-2001||06:19 PM||||The '84 lower ball joints are welded in, all others are press fit. If you have an '84, you may consider upgrading to the later control arms to get a slightly tighter turning radius and serviceable ball joints.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jncomutt||11-18-2001||08:44 PM||||Hey Rockcrawl, thanks for posting that info.  My 86 parts car is goin away in 3 days, I better hurry up and get the control arms off for my 84 b4 its gone..!||||reg||1
Z||000007||ITB 2M4||11-19-2001||12:33 AM||||The MOOG problem solver ball joints are the way to go.  They are slotted, so you can adjust the camber if you wanted.  I've got the part number, but it's out in my truck right now.  I'll go out and get it tomorrow morning.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
